// The two ways a new string comes into existence: one byte of it, or two
// existing ones end to end.
//
// Both hand back arena space from the size-class pool, which str_free gives
// back when the compiler proves the string owned and dead (docs/MEMORY.md 2a);
// a string it cannot prove stays until the program ends. Each block is the
// count, the bytes, and a NUL; the string is the address of the bytes.

chr(int n) {
  word p = str_blk(1);
  st64(p, 1);
  string ret_s = chr_term(p, n);
} return string ret_s;

// `copy_bytes` answers with the count it copied, so the address just past each
// half falls out of the copy rather than being computed twice.
concat(string s, string r) {
  word p = concat_alloc(s, r);
  string ret_s = concat_at(s, r, p);
} return string ret_s;

chr_term(word p, int n) {
  st8(p + 8, n);
  st8(p + 9, 0);
  string ret_s = w2s(p + 8);
} return string ret_s;
